Title: Fuse the cosine loop: upstream's vector scan pays 2,2x for a redundant traversal and a polymorphic call site
Created: 2026-08-29
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-08-29T00:00Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-08-29T00:00Z Minh Ha-Duong note Author authorized a third in-flight upstream PR slot for this work; recorded in DECISIONS.md
2026-08-29T00:00Z Minh Ha-Duong note Built on the fork at branch fuse-cosine-loop (999cb1c); suite, typecheck and lint green; 2,19x measured against dist/

--- body ---
## Context

Upstream issue [#30](https://github.com/oscardvs/zoteus/issues/30) (Michael
Logies) reports `zotero_semantic_search` at 90–105 s per query on a 255 703-passage
index at 3072 dims, against ~13 s for a ChromaDB-backed competitor. His diagnosis is
correct and measured: memory stays flat at ~110 MB, so the cost is a streaming,
CPU-bound, O(N) scan in JavaScript, not an all-vectors-in-RAM problem.

Reading that scan turned up a defect independent of the issue's own suggested
directions. `cosine()` in `sqlite-index.ts` traverses each stored vector **twice** —
once inside `norm(b)`, once for the dot product — and the `norm()` it calls is shared
with the query-side call at `vectorSearch`, which passes a `number[]` where every row
passes a `Float32Array`. One `number[]` observation makes that call site polymorphic
for the life of the process, and it never recovers. The `ArrayLike<number>` signature
is the tell.

Both are removed by accumulating the dot product and the row's squared norm in one
pass. The result is bit-identical: the same operations in the same order, so no
rebuild, no migration, no test churn.

Measured by `bench/cosine_fusion.mjs`, results in
`bench/results/0070-cosine-fusion/`, Node v22.23.1 on Linux, five repetitions.

Arithmetic in isolation, 3072 dims, per row (`--shapes`):

| shape | µs/row |
|---|---|
| two-pass, shared `norm()` — upstream today | 23,90 |
| two-pass, monomorphic `norm()` | 12,76 |
| fused single traversal | 6,34 |

**3,77x**, decomposing into **1,87x** from the polymorphic call site and **2,01x**
from the redundant traversal. The middle row is the controlled comparison: same two
passes, only the callee's monomorphism differs.

In situ (`--insitu`), calling the *shipped* function out of a built `dist/`, at the
issue's exact geometry — 255 703 rows, 3072 dims, a 3,4 GB fixture — with the two
scans interleaved:

| | median | µs/row |
|---|---|---|
| two-pass (before) | 8 606 ms | 33,66 |
| fused (shipped) | 3 933 ms | 15,38 |
| iterate + decode, no arithmetic | 2 559 ms | 10,01 |

**2,19x median**, and 2,16x worst case — comparing the fastest before against the
slowest after — with no dependency. Underneath sits an unmoved row fetch, which is
why the in-situ figure is below the isolated 3,77x.

Equivalence is checked over the whole store rather than a fixture: 255 703 rows
checked, 0 mismatches, top-15 ids and scores identical.

Two measurement traps caught here, both worth remembering, and both encoded in the
driver so the next run cannot repeat them. A first harness ran both implementations
through one `scan(f)` call site, which made *that* site polymorphic and blocked
inlining for both, reading 1,61x where two direct call sites read 2,48x. And single
runs disagreed by ±15%, enough to invent a 2,9x that repetition does not support —
the fused path is stable to ±1% while the two-pass baseline, doing more work, swings
several times that.

One hypothesis was tested and refuted: `vector` is the last column of `passages`,
after the passage `text`, so `SELECT id, vector` should have to read through the text
to reach it — measured at 1,00x against a narrow id+vector table. The 12 KB vector
dominates the overflow chain.

`vector-store.ts` (memory backend) carries the same two-pass shape. Its `norm()` is
monomorphic — both operands are `number[]` — so it gains the traversal half only,
~2,0x. One idiom, both backends, no other file touched: the shape of PR #19.

**What this ticket does not claim.** It does not close #30. The scan measured here
is 8,6 s where he reports ~95 s, an 11x gap on his Windows/Node 24 machine that
nothing measured here explains, and whose cause decides this change's value to him:
a uniform CPU-side slowdown carries the ratio and puts him near 43 s, while a purely
I/O-bound gap leaves the saving at ~5 s of 95. The one experiment that settles it is
his, and it is one line — whether a second identical query, run immediately, is
faster than the first. Say so in the PR body rather than letting the number imply a
fix.

**Contingent on direction.** Under the issue's option 1 (`sqlite-vec` `vec0`) the JS
cosine is deleted outright and this work is discarded. It is worth landing only if
the maintainer does not go that way.

## Process note

This PR exceeds the ratified two-in-flight cap (#27 and #28 both open). The author
authorized a third slot on 2026-08-29 on the grounds that it is a clear code
simplification; the ruling is recorded in DECISIONS.md, which is authoritative.

## Relevant files

- `src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts` — `cosine()`, `norm()`, `vectorSearch()`;
  the two-pass traversal and the polymorphic call site
- `src/features/search/vector-store.ts` — `cosine()`, `norm()`; the same two-pass
  shape on the memory backend
- `bench/` (this repo) — the drivers, once promoted with their results JSON and
  figure-guard declarations

## Actions

1. Fuse `cosine()` in `sqlite-index.ts` to a single traversal accumulating both the
   dot product and the row's squared norm; drop the row-side call to `norm()` so
   that call site sees only `number[]`.
2. Same fusion in `vector-store.ts`.
3. Promote the measurement drivers into `bench/` with a results JSON under
   `bench/results/`, and declare every figure quoted above in `bench/check_figures.py`.
4. Open the upstream PR from the author's fork, with a body that states the measured
   2,19x, the refuted wide-row hypothesis, and explicitly that this does not close #30.

## Test

- Red step: a case asserting `cosine` agrees with a two-pass reference to the last
  bit over a fixture of non-unit vectors — it must pass before and after, since the
  change is bit-identical, and it is what makes that claim mechanical rather than
  asserted.
- Upstream's existing vector-search suite must pass unchanged on both backends.

## Verification

- [x] `npm test` (752 passed, 7 skipped), `tsc --noEmit`, and lint clean in the fork
- [x] Fused and two-pass cosine agree bit-for-bit on a non-unit-vector fixture, on
      both backends, including zero rows, both directions of width mismatch, and NaN
      propagation — 7 cases, `tests/features/vector-cosine-equivalence.test.ts`
- [x] The test is a detector, not a decoration: deleting the width-mismatch path
      fails it, and fails exactly the intended case
- [x] Equivalence over the whole store, not a fixture: 255 703 rows, 0 mismatches,
      top-15 ranking and scores identical
- [x] Re-measured in situ against the built `dist/`: 2,19x median
- [x] `make check` green in this repo (figure guard declares every number above)

## Invariants

- Ranking output is unchanged — this is a pure-refactor claim and is validated by
  byte-comparing results, not by a green suite alone.
- No new dependency, no schema change, no index rebuild.

## Exit criteria

- The upstream PR is open, its body carries the measurement and the explicit
  non-claim about #30, and DECISIONS.md records the third-slot authorization.
